{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t02-003",
  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "ASP.NET Core middleware pipeline order and branching",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "The request pipeline is an ordered chain of delegates. Middleware can run before and after the next delegate, short-circuit the request, or terminate the pipeline; response processing unwinds in reverse order.",
    "Exception handling belongs early enough to catch downstream failures. Routing establishes the selected endpoint, and authentication, authorization, CORS, rate limiting, caching, static files, and endpoint execution have ordering constraints.",
    "WebApplication automatically adds some middleware in certain configurations. Explicit calls and framework defaults together determine the actual pipeline, so developers must understand what is added and avoid assuming every middleware runs in a desired position.",
    "Branching can isolate paths or predicates. Map changes the path seen within the branch; MapWhen selects by a predicate and does not rejoin; UseWhen selects by a predicate and normally rejoins unless the branch terminates.",
    "Custom middleware should use the request delegate model, preserve cancellation and exception behavior, avoid capturing scoped services in singleton middleware constructors, and not consume request bodies without restoring or buffering them appropriately."
  ],
